@page "/agents"
@using Scheduler.Web.Identity
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@* Static: a page to read, linked from the home page, the header and the voting page. The MCP URL comes from the
   request, like McpResource, so it is right on every host. See docs/design.md, "Web UI". *@

<PageTitle>Use Scheduler with your agent</PageTitle>

<article class="agent-guide">
<h1>Use Scheduler with your agent</h1>

<p>
    You can do everything here from the web pages and the links in Scheduler's emails. Connect your AI agent
    (Claude, GitHub Copilot, RockBot, or any other assistant that speaks MCP) and it can do most of the work for you:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>When you're invited:</strong> find the requests waiting for your answer, check every proposed time
        against your calendar, suggest answers for you to confirm, vote, and suggest other times when none work.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>When you organize:</strong> find times you're free, start the poll and email the invitations, watch the
        answers come in, recommend the best time, then put the meeting in your calendar.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="calendar">Your agent needs your calendar</h2>

<p>
    Scheduler never sees anyone's calendar. That's on purpose: nobody shares their calendar with the server or with
    each other, and the server only learns the answers. It also means the checking happens in <em>your</em> agent.
    An agent that can't read your calendar can only guess, or ask you about every time.
</p>

<p>
    So connect your agent to every calendar that decides when you're free: a work calendar and a personal one, if you
    have both. A clash in any of them counts. Organizers also want their agent to be able to create events, so the
    chosen time lands in the calendar.
</p>

<p>
    Many agents already have this: Claude has Google Calendar and Microsoft 365 connectors, for example. If yours
    doesn't, or your calendars are spread across several accounts,
    <a href="https://github.com/MarimerLLC/calendar-mcp">Adjutant</a> can provide it. Adjutant is an open-source MCP
    server you run yourself. It gives an agent email, calendar and contacts across Microsoft 365, Outlook.com, Google
    Workspace and IMAP accounts at once, and lets it find Scheduler's invitation emails too.
</p>

<h2 id="connect">Connect your agent</h2>

<p>Add this server to your agent as an MCP server (some agents call it a connector):</p>

<pre><code>@McpUrl</code></pre>

<p>Your agent has to sign in as you, in one of two ways.</p>

<h3 id="oauth">Sign in through your browser (OAuth)</h3>

<p>
    For agents that can open a browser, which is most of them. The first time the agent connects, it opens a page here
    where you sign in with Microsoft or Google (or an emailed link) and allow the agent to act for you. After that it
    stays signed in. No token to copy, nothing to store.
</p>

<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Claude</strong> (claude.ai, the desktop and mobile apps): in Settings, Connectors, add a custom connector
        with the URL above, then connect it.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Claude Code:</strong> <code>claude mcp add --transport http scheduler @McpUrl</code>, then sign in from
        <code>/mcp</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>VS Code with GitHub Copilot:</strong> add it to <code>mcp.json</code>:
        <pre><code>@VsCodeEntry</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>Any other MCP client that supports OAuth sign-in works the same way.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="token">Use a personal access token</h3>

<p>
    For agents that can't open a browser: one that runs unattended on a server, like RockBot, or a script. Create a
    token on your <a href="/account">account page</a> and give it to your agent, which sends it with every request as
    <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> followed by the token. A token acts as you until it expires or you revoke it, so
    treat it like a password and give each agent its own.
</p>

<h2 id="use">Then just ask</h2>

<p>Once your agent is connected, ask in your own words. For example:</p>

<ul>
    <li>"Do I have any scheduling requests waiting? Check them against my calendar and suggest answers."</li>
    <li>"Find a time next week for a 30-minute meeting with alice@example.com and bob@example.com."</li>
    <li>"How are the votes going on my planning meeting? Which time looks best?"</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Scheduler also offers two ready-made prompts, <code>handle_scheduling_requests</code> and
    <code>schedule_meeting</code>, if your agent lets you pick prompts from its servers.
</p>

<p class="hint">
    Anyone can answer an invitation. To start polls of your own, <a href="/ask-to-organize">ask to organize</a> (or ask
    your agent to). Invited at another address? Add it on your <a href="/account">account page</a> so you, and your agent,
    can answer for it.
</p>
</article>

@code {
    private string McpUrl => Navigation.ToAbsoluteUri(McpResource.Path).AbsoluteUri;

    private string VsCodeEntry => $$"""
        {
          "servers": {
            "scheduler": { "type": "http", "url": "{{McpUrl}}" }
          }
        }
        """;
}
